WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.500
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here, and
today I wanna talk about something very

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:10.020
important and that is that language
earning is not that complicated.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.240
The reason I wanna talk about that is
because I was going through my library

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.900
here, uh, in the section where I have
books on language acquisition, language

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.270
learning, many of which I bought 20 years
ago when we were getting started with

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:29.400
LingQ, and I was reading up on the subject
from attending conferences and so forth.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:35.040
And I went back through some of these
books and I realized just how complicated

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:42.390
the sort of language acquisition academics
make the subject of a language journey.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.340
It's really not that complicated.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.560
And so I wanted to go through with you
some of the books that I have and some of

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.620
the concepts that appear in these books.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.730
You know, learning book, Second
Language Acquisition, Rod Ellis.

00:00:55.745 --> 00:00:59.150
Vocabulary: Description,
Acquisition and Pedagogy.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.739
Learning Vocabulary in Another Language.

00:01:02.029 --> 00:01:06.990
Vocabulary in Language Teaching,
uh, you know, Applied Linguistics,

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:09.030
uh, I mean there's lots.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:09.270
Okay.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:10.590
I even have a Swedish one.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.050
I mean, I used to wherever I was, and
I would go into a bookstore and, and

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.759
see what there was on this subject.

00:01:18.509 --> 00:01:24.090
And they make it so complex because
what the language acquisition experts,

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:29.595
with the exception of Stephen Krashed,
what they try to do is describe...

00:01:29.715 --> 00:01:34.545
it's as if language learning is a
motion picture, and they're going to

00:01:34.545 --> 00:01:41.655
describe it frame by frame, uh, or even
not a motion picture, but uh, animated

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:47.505
cartoon where every little step,
every little possibility is explained.

00:01:47.685 --> 00:01:52.575
Whereas actually it's a very
simple, natural phenomenon.

00:01:52.875 --> 00:01:58.620
It's the process of acquiring new
language habits, largely through a lot

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:04.290
of listening at reading and the kind,
for example, uh, I don't wanna pick on

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:09.150
this one because this Rod Ellis Second
Language Acquisition is in many ways

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:14.640
the best of all these books because
it's short and sweet, not as good as

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.890
Krashen, who has a number of very short
books explaining language acquisition.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:24.810
But some of the things he refers to here
and I jotted some of them down so that

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.180
I could more easily go through them.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:32.010
So, uh, first of all, he goes to
great length to explain that the

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:35.040
order of acquisition depends on
the learner's native language.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:39.450
Again, that's obvious if you don't
have articles in your own language,

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.230
articles in English or in languages
which have articles are going to be

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:48.870
more difficult to learn, uh, if you
don't have plurals in your own language,

00:02:49.215 --> 00:02:53.595
plurals, which would be absolutely easy
for someone who speaks a language that

00:02:53.595 --> 00:02:59.565
has plurals, is that's gonna be a lot
more difficult to get right each time.

00:02:59.565 --> 00:03:04.695
If you're not used to the idea that
every plural noun, you know, might

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.295
have to add an s or do something
that indicates that it's plural.

00:03:07.875 --> 00:03:09.734
So it's an obvious thing.

00:03:10.185 --> 00:03:16.215
Uh, again, he goes into all the
different kinds of context that

00:03:16.274 --> 00:03:18.495
influence how a learner learns.

00:03:18.495 --> 00:03:23.265
The linguistic context, the situational
context, the psycho-linguistic context,

00:03:23.415 --> 00:03:30.644
the form function mappings, fossilization,
all of these things that affect how the

00:03:30.644 --> 00:03:36.225
process whereby a learner learns, but it
doesn't influence how the learner learns.

00:03:36.435 --> 00:03:39.885
The learner still has to learn the
same way through massive input,

00:03:40.035 --> 00:03:41.954
through massive listening and reading.

00:03:41.984 --> 00:03:47.460
Talks about learner language and the
properties of learner language and

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.020
how, again, you know, the forms of
the kinds of errors that learner makes

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.000
are influenced by all of these things.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:04:00.800
Um, then he talks about, you know,
social distance and he gives an example

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.530
of, of a, perhaps I think it was a
Japanese person learning English.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:09.630
The teacher made reference to Bart Simpson
of The Simpsons, and she didn't know

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:13.860
what that was, and that then created
the social distance for her between the

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:19.500
language she was learning English and you
know, not feeling part of that culture.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.410
Well, of course that's always
a part of language acquisition.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:26.670
You're acquiring a language that's
initially quite distant, eventually

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:31.470
gets closer as you get closer to the
language and as you engage with content,

00:04:32.745 --> 00:04:36.255
cultural aspects of that language,
it becomes less and less distant.

00:04:36.465 --> 00:04:40.425
But along the way, there are going to
be things that remind you that you are

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:43.785
trying to get into this other culture.

00:04:43.815 --> 00:04:50.294
Talks about different discourse rules
that, uh, you know, in American society

00:04:50.294 --> 00:04:55.875
if, if someone makes a compliment, then
the response is quite a lengthy one.

00:04:55.875 --> 00:04:58.724
Oh, you have a nice shirt and you're
supposed to, according to him, you're

00:04:58.724 --> 00:05:01.515
supposed to say, well, you know,
I've gotten, my mother gave it to

00:05:01.515 --> 00:05:03.135
me, or some lengthy explanation.

00:05:03.434 --> 00:05:05.325
Whereas I'm not sure
that's obviously the case.

00:05:05.594 --> 00:05:08.775
The danger when you try to make
generalizations is, in fact,

00:05:08.924 --> 00:05:10.875
very often that's not the case.

00:05:11.205 --> 00:05:14.054
And if someone says to me, you've
got a nice shirt, I say, thanks.

00:05:14.475 --> 00:05:17.475
I don't get into a lengthy
explanation of where I got the shirt.

00:05:17.475 --> 00:05:20.775
Terms like speech acts and speech events.

00:05:20.775 --> 00:05:25.305
Then they get into this whole critical
period hypothesis that we are best able

00:05:25.305 --> 00:05:29.715
to learn languages when we're young
before the age of 10 or 12, which is

00:05:29.715 --> 00:05:36.225
true because the brain has not yet
coalesced and, and and kind of hardened

00:05:36.225 --> 00:05:38.985
if you want around the native language.

00:05:38.985 --> 00:05:42.255
And we could just as easily
learn any language and later on

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:43.335
it's a little more difficult.

00:05:43.665 --> 00:05:46.065
But then again, there's not
much we can do with that.

00:05:46.065 --> 00:05:50.325
If I'm a 20 year old learning a language,
whatever are the conditions that affect

00:05:50.325 --> 00:05:52.455
a 20 year old, that's gonna affect me.

00:05:52.545 --> 00:05:56.655
It's not that useful to me as a language
learner to know that I should have learned

00:05:56.655 --> 00:05:59.115
the language, that language at age eight.

00:05:59.685 --> 00:06:03.015
Uh, he even goes on in Ellis's book.

00:06:03.015 --> 00:06:05.385
He says, you know, integrative motivation.

00:06:05.385 --> 00:06:09.375
He says, for example, uh, English
Canadians learning French are very

00:06:09.375 --> 00:06:13.695
motivated to learn French because they
want to integrate into the French society.

00:06:13.695 --> 00:06:15.284
Well, that's absolutely not the case.

00:06:15.555 --> 00:06:19.195
Most English Canadians learning French
are not at all motivated to learn French.

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:23.775
Some are, and those that are, they
learn, but others learn it as a

00:06:23.775 --> 00:06:28.350
subject uh, that has to be learned
just like, uh, is the case I think

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:31.620
with, uh, language learners in many
countries, it's a subject they're not

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:33.870
necessarily that interested in learning.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.330
Those that are motivated do learn.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:41.310
Uh, it talks about negotiating
meaning all these terms.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:43.800
Uh, so, uh, that's Ellis.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:48.750
So I, I gave him particular, uh,
attention here, but you know, again,

00:06:48.755 --> 00:06:51.660
I've sort of dogeared some pages.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:57.974
So here he talks about, Uh, you
know, it's important to allow, uh,

00:06:58.005 --> 00:07:00.044
students to select books themselves.

00:07:00.044 --> 00:07:00.974
Well, yes.

00:07:01.185 --> 00:07:01.755
There you go.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.135
That's a good thing to say.

00:07:03.554 --> 00:07:08.955
Um, you know, things that affect the
ability to learn a word, what makes

00:07:08.955 --> 00:07:11.145
it difficult, uh, how it's spelled.

00:07:11.145 --> 00:07:11.655
Yes.

00:07:11.655 --> 00:07:15.164
If you're learning Spanish and, uh,
there's a one-to-one relationship

00:07:15.164 --> 00:07:18.825
between how it's spelled and how it's
pronounced, that's gonna be a lot

00:07:18.825 --> 00:07:20.445
easier than if you're learning English.

00:07:20.534 --> 00:07:22.745
Not to mention, uh, Arabic.

00:07:23.625 --> 00:07:27.405
Length, longer words are more difficult,
you know, four or five chapters and

00:07:27.405 --> 00:07:29.505
why it's harder to learn those words.

00:07:29.805 --> 00:07:33.765
Uh, all of these things
that are relatively obvious.

00:07:34.545 --> 00:07:37.605
You know, if learners do not know,
or they must discover its meaning

00:07:37.605 --> 00:07:40.725
by guessing from their structural
knowledge of the language.

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:44.595
Guessing from an L1 cognate,
guessing from context.

00:07:44.925 --> 00:07:46.395
Well, they don't
necessarily have to guess.

00:07:46.395 --> 00:07:47.295
I don't guess.

00:07:47.505 --> 00:07:53.625
If I'm reading online, I just look it up
and, uh, I know full well that looking

00:07:53.625 --> 00:07:55.485
it up, I won't remember that word.

00:07:55.545 --> 00:07:58.335
How on LingQ, of course, the
blue word becomes yellow.

00:07:58.455 --> 00:08:02.595
The next time I see it, I'll know that
I've seen that word before, but I'll

00:08:02.595 --> 00:08:04.635
look it up again and again and again.

00:08:04.785 --> 00:08:06.795
And gradually those words become known.

00:08:07.005 --> 00:08:13.095
So some of this, uh, material is sort
of predates the advent of the internet.

00:08:13.275 --> 00:08:17.685
Paul Nation is an expert on language
and particularly vocabulary acquisition.

00:08:20.055 --> 00:08:25.715
When, uh, so noticing involves
decontextualization, decontextualization

00:08:25.785 --> 00:08:29.535
occurs when learners give attention to a
language item as a part of the language

00:08:29.535 --> 00:08:33.164
rather than as part of a sentence, or
excuse me, part of a, of a message.

00:08:33.885 --> 00:08:39.375
I don't know that I deliberately
decontextualize if I see a word in

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:43.815
different context, eventually the
sense of that word grows on me.

00:08:44.265 --> 00:08:47.295
While listening or reading a learner
notices that a word is new or thinks

00:08:47.355 --> 00:08:51.495
I've seen that word before, or thinks
that word is used differently from

00:08:51.495 --> 00:08:52.815
the ways I've seen it used before.

00:08:53.085 --> 00:08:57.165
First of all, if you're not on a system
like LingQ, you may not even know

00:08:57.165 --> 00:08:58.545
that you've seen that word before.

00:08:58.635 --> 00:09:02.145
Learners negotiate the meaning
of a word with each other.

00:09:02.145 --> 00:09:06.345
Well, that's a, again, on the assumption
as is often the case with people

00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:08.055
who talk about language acquisition.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:11.520
Um, the assumption is that people
are sitting in a classroom.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:16.320
Creative or generative use, a third
major process that may lead to a

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.390
word being remembered is generation.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:21.870
Increasing number of studies that
show that generative processing,

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:23.970
uh, is an important factor.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:28.290
Generative processing occurs when
previously met words are subsequently

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:33.090
met or used in ways that differ from
the previous meeting with the word.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:33.840
Yeah.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:37.770
That's something that occurs
naturally as you read more and more.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:40.080
The more you read, the more you listen.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:43.560
The more you encounter words in
different contexts, the more this kind

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:46.440
of generative processing takes place.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:49.780
He also recommends spending time on words.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:51.000
Yeah.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:54.750
The more time you spend on one word,
the less time you have to spend on

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:59.040
acquiring more words and exposing
yourself to more of the language.

00:09:59.385 --> 00:10:03.855
That again, can be a function of what
you like to do and how you like to learn.

00:10:04.305 --> 00:10:09.135
Here is again, norberd Schmidt, who
is an expert on acquiring vocabulary.

00:10:10.305 --> 00:10:13.095
Again, he, the majority of
words, do not have a one-to-one

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:15.525
relationship with a single referrent.

00:10:16.605 --> 00:10:16.955
Yeah.

00:10:17.115 --> 00:10:18.955
Words don't just mean one theme.

00:10:19.485 --> 00:10:25.935
Words can mean a variety of things, but
here again, a natural process of massive

00:10:25.965 --> 00:10:30.840
input, massive reading and listening,
encountering words in different contexts

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:36.450
gradually help you sort of fill out
the full scope of the meaning of words.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:39.990
Talks about word associations, that
we would learn the word needle,

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:44.220
and then typically associated with
thread or pins or sharp or so.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:44.970
Sure.

00:10:44.975 --> 00:10:46.320
These are things that happen naturally.

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:53.110
I'm not sure much is gained by sort of
explaining, uh, what does he say here?

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:58.350
Some explicit learning is probably
necessary to reach a vocabulary size

00:10:58.439 --> 00:11:04.560
threshold that enables incidental
learning from reading, uh, uh, my

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:08.790
experience is that for the longest
time there continue to be so many

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:14.640
unknown words, that it's unpleasant to
rely totally on the ability to infer.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:19.770
And I prefer, uh, learning on LingQ,
looking up every word, putting it in

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:22.260
my database, coming across it again.

00:11:22.574 --> 00:11:27.464
And then eventually seeing it become
part of my at least passive vocabulary,

00:11:27.734 --> 00:11:31.755
which is the first step towards making
it part of your active vocabulary.

00:11:31.814 --> 00:11:34.245
So I'm not gonna go through all of these.

00:11:34.275 --> 00:11:36.675
I've got applied linguistics here.

00:11:37.064 --> 00:11:40.614
Uh, you know how languages are
learned, language teaching methodology,

00:11:41.265 --> 00:11:46.125
but it is full of this effort
to complicate something that is

00:11:46.125 --> 00:11:47.895
essentially quite straightforward.

00:11:48.314 --> 00:11:50.594
And I would refer everyone
to Stephen Krashen.

00:11:51.225 --> 00:11:54.074
I don't have to agree with
everything Stephen Krashen says.

00:11:54.105 --> 00:11:56.564
Uh, he obviously is much
more expert than I am.

00:11:56.564 --> 00:11:58.365
I draw on my own experience.

00:11:58.365 --> 00:12:04.454
He draws on research, but essentially,
uh, it's a process of, uh, and

00:12:04.459 --> 00:12:05.895
I would encourage everyone.

00:12:05.895 --> 00:12:09.525
In fact, I will leave a link to
Stephen Krashen's website, uh,

00:12:09.525 --> 00:12:14.345
the natural learning approach,
the um, input hypothesis.

00:12:15.695 --> 00:12:20.565
A number of, you know, very clear
and simple explanations of how we

00:12:20.565 --> 00:12:24.225
learn, which basically comes down
to lots of listening and reading.

00:12:24.495 --> 00:12:29.025
And when you have acquired a certain
vocabulary, uh, then you start

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:31.155
speaking with more and more confidence.

00:12:31.425 --> 00:12:35.565
The greater your, uh, comprehension
ability, the greater your vocabulary,

00:12:35.565 --> 00:12:40.185
the more confident you feel when
speaking, and if you are more

00:12:40.185 --> 00:12:44.430
confident, you will speak better and
everything will gradually improve.

00:12:44.459 --> 00:12:45.600
It's that simple.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:47.400
It's not more complicated than that.

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:52.949
And we like to think that at LingQ we
facilitate this process of learning

00:12:52.949 --> 00:12:58.980
through massive input by providing sort
of training wheels initially with, uh,

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:02.610
easier content like our mini stories
where there's a lot of repetition

00:13:02.939 --> 00:13:08.220
and then the ability to expand into
more and more difficult content,

00:13:08.225 --> 00:13:10.500
content that's of greater interest.

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:15.720
As long as you keep doing that, you are
going to gradually learn the language.

00:13:15.959 --> 00:13:22.110
The emphasis is on the word gradually,
and those people who are motivated

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:26.760
enough to learn on their own, uh, and
who feel confident enough, which isn't

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:30.350
everyone to go out there and learn on
their own and choose content of their

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:35.520
own to listen to and aren't necessarily
waiting for a teacher to spoon feed them,

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:38.339
those people are going to learn better.

00:13:38.805 --> 00:13:39.705
With or without...

00:13:39.765 --> 00:13:42.195
that's not to say that a
teacher can't be helpful.

00:13:42.435 --> 00:13:46.995
A teacher can be a tremendous source of
encouragement and stimulus and advice,

00:13:47.385 --> 00:13:53.025
but, uh, there are many things that as
learners we can do to supplement and

00:13:53.025 --> 00:13:58.665
compliment what the teacher can provide
us with, or just to go out and fend

00:13:58.665 --> 00:14:01.095
for ourselves and gradually develop.

00:14:01.515 --> 00:14:04.875
Uh, you know, an ability and
an understanding of languages

00:14:05.145 --> 00:14:06.464
that are of interest to us.

00:14:06.795 --> 00:14:10.964
So thank you that I just kind of felt
that when I was looking through my

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:13.095
library I say, wow, it's that complicated.

00:14:13.334 --> 00:14:14.175
I don't think so.

00:14:14.475 --> 00:14:15.824
Anyway, thanks for listening.

00:14:16.214 --> 00:14:16.845
Bye for now.

